I think this raises a very interesting point, the bit about "As if he'd be
alive."

I consider myself a very rational person. In fact, at least two girlfriends
have criticized me as being "hyper-rational." (My response to them: "That
is not logical.") And yet I can readily see that the rational course of
action, if I want to increase my survival odds, is for me to do several
things:

1. Get rid of all guns, stop talking about the Feds, and live a meek,
normal life. (This way I won't come to the attention of anyone in power. I
agree completely that any shoot-out with the Feds is doomed.)

2. Get off this and other lists, which are probably the subjects of
occasional probes by Feds and their ilk (the Cypherpunks list has had
strange things sent to it from "*.army.mil" sites, and several sysadmins
are getting orders to shut down anonymous remailers.)

3. "Be a good German." That is, say and do nothing controversial.

>From a survival point of view, becoming a milquetoast, a Walter Mitty type,
is definitely the way to go.

So why, in my own way, do I continue to live near the edge? The surge of
adrenaline in poking a stick in Big Brother's eye? Nietzsche's "Live
dangerously"?

Maybe our mememeister, Keith Henson, can explain this in terms of the
survival value of certain kinds of belief systems.

Many on this list delight in riding motorcycles ( = donorcycles) at very
high speeds on twisting mountain roads. (Not me!) But perhaps these
behaviors are related.

Any thoughts?

-Tim
