to increase his risk, a cryonics/anti-religion meme causes Keith to 
increase his risk, etc. beyond what would be in the "rational 
self-interest" of individual survival.

(b) This is a form of reciprocal altruism, if Keith increases his 
likelihood of other cryonicists risking their own livelihood, lives, 
etc. to suspend him properly, protect him while suspended, and revive 
him in the future.  This kind of altruism is of little use unless
the altruist brags about it, and indeed Keith has not refrained from
letting us know about the risks he has undertaken, and in turn the 
many people who have decided they do not want to risk their own 
potential vast longevity to increase the chances for another, have not 
found it necessary say so.  (In fairness to the both sides, I note that 
this decision is highly dependent on what one thinks the odds of 
revival are, what the odds of infection are, etc. not just on the 
altruistic motives or lack thereof).

(c) genes have built the basic human motivations of many, especially
young males, to seek out risky&exciting activities, such as fighting, 
climbing mountians, riding "donorcycles", etc. because in the
hunter-gatherer mileau under which most human genes evolved, 
activities with similar sensations increased the probability of
gene propagation.  One specific example are young males sent
off to steal women from neighboring tribes in many h-g cultures,
and indeed in many more modern cultures recorded in history, and
in history/myths like the Trojan War, and in chimpanzees.  Economics 
as a motivator of war is a rather modern "civilized" development.

A problem with (b) is that there is plenty of room for "cheaters"
who do not risk anything beyond their life-insurance and
sign-up fees, and their chances of getting a good suspension may
not be significantly worse than Keith's.  These people might 
reasonably argue that this is individualistic virtue, not "cheating".  

So, are we seeing selfish memes masquerading under the banner of 
individualism, with some major exceptions where convenient, or is 
there a genuine positive payoff for the individual despite the 
risks?

I haven't resolved these dilemnas to my own satisfaction, so I
haven't settled on any of the above as a dominant explantion,
but I do find the subject of individualistic altruism (down 
Objectivists! Down! :-) mighty fascinating.

