Date:     Fri, 5 Mar 93 9:11:08 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Justice, Dale Worley, & Libertarian Foundations

Dale Worley made a claim which I paraphrase: "Libertarianism is based upon
the child's cry that 'I don't wanna!'"

This is patently offensive.  Since it doesn't seem to be an offense with a
personal victim (that is, he wasn't commenting about any personality), it's
merely incredibly insulting, or a class-action offense.

If he doesn't publicly apologize, I invite readers to join me in a class-
action suit against him for the offense.  I won't say anything more about
this unless I decide to publicly call for additional parties to the suit,
to try to avoid provoking flames.

This claim follows in a long series of cliches from Worley about what
libertarianism does and doesn't consist of, mostly false.  The latest of
these is the claim that we oppose "fairness," and want it abolished.

Fairness is synomymous with justice.  I love justice.  I expect that many
but not all of the rest of us do, too.  I seek a more just society.  Thus,
my belief in libertarianism follows from my belief in fairness: I seek
maximum equal liberty for all because I think it just.

There is an alternative theory of justice whose ideal is equality of condition.
Due to the fact that few people actually believe in this, its proponents, who
dominate the intelligentsia, cloak it in vague language in order to misrepresent
themselves to people.  They say they favor "fairness" in the sense of cutting
the tall down to the size of the short, the rich down to the size of the poor,
without being explicit, and so corrupt language with corrupt ideas, as Orwell
recognized decades ago.

Since these theories of justice are mutually exclusive, proponents of one
seek to abolish the other except as historical curiosity.

